Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "american-orbital-vehicle-program": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"American Orbital Vehicle Program",category:"Space programs",eyebrow:"Operational Vehicles · OV series · 1969 onward",infoboxKicker:"Runway-to-orbit transportation system",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/american-spaceplane-lineage.svg",alt:"Lineage diagram of American Pathfinder, Columbia, Liberty, Hercules, Ranger, Enterprise, and Zenith orbital aircraft",caption:"The OV lineage separates experimental proof, lunar flight, routine transport, heavy lift, servicing, and military security"},
    lead:"The American Orbital Vehicle Program is the family of self-powered, horizontal-takeoff and horizontal-landing aerospace planes operated by the United States Aerospace Force. The program makes reusable piloted aircraft rather than expendable ballistic launchers the public and operational center of American spaceflight.",
    canon:"The runway-to-orbit architecture, Aerospace Force ownership, vehicle sequence, principal roles, and milestone years are established. Exact masses, payloads, propulsion-cycle details, airframe totals by subtype, and complete fleet registers remain open pending revision of the specialist aerospace reference.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Origins in the German satellite shock",html:"<p>Germany's 1957 <em>Raumbote I</em> launch forces Washington to treat high-speed aviation, missiles, computing, and orbit as one national problem. American X-plane, ramjet, lifting-body, turbine, and rocket research is consolidated around a deliberately aviation-centered answer.</p><p>The political promise is that Americans will fly into space and return under their own wings rather than ride as passengers inside a ballistic shell.</p>"},
      {id:"architecture",title:"Integrated runway-to-orbit architecture",html:"<p>Operational vehicles accelerate from exceptionally long runways under integrated multi-cycle propulsion. Turbine and ramjet or dual-mode ramjet operation gives way to onboard hydrogen–oxygen rockets as the atmosphere thins. The same airframe enters orbit, maneuvers, reenters, and lands horizontally.</p><p>Wings, landing gear, inlets, cooling, and thermal protection impose severe mass penalties. America accepts them in exchange for recovery, flexible basing, frequent inspection, and an aviation-like operating culture.</p>"},
      {id:"lineage",title:"The operational lineage",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Vehicle</th><th>Entry</th><th>Principal role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href='ov-1a-pathfinder.html'>OV-1A Pathfinder</a></td><td>1969</td><td>Proof of runway-to-orbit flight</td></tr><tr><td><a href='columbia-spaceplane-program.html'>OV-2A/2B Columbia</a></td><td>1971</td><td>Lunar landing and orbital refueling</td></tr><tr><td><a href='liberty-spaceplane.html'>OV-3A/3B Liberty</a></td><td>1972/1974</td><td>Routine crew, cargo, and automatic freight</td></tr><tr><td><a href='ov-4a-hercules.html'>OV-4A Hercules</a></td><td>1976</td><td>Heavy modules, trusses, propellant, bulk cargo</td></tr><tr><td><a href='ov-5a-ranger.html'>OV-5A Ranger</a></td><td>1979</td><td>Inspection, retrieval, repair, and rescue</td></tr><tr><td><a href='ov-6a-enterprise.html'>OV-6A Enterprise</a></td><td>1983</td><td>High-frequency second-generation transport</td></tr><tr><td><a href='project-zenith.html'>OSV-1A Zenith</a></td><td>1984</td><td>Military inspection and orbital security</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"commands",title:"Commands and operating authority",html:"<p>Aerospace Development Command manages experimental vehicles and procurement. Orbital Transportation Command operates the general fleet, aerospace fields, traffic control, and routine station movement. Space Science and Development Command directs scientific destinations, while Orbital Security Command controls Zenith and protected missions.</p><p>The United States has no independent NASA-like civil operator. Universities, firms, and foreign researchers fly inside a military transportation and safety system.</p>"},
      {id:"fields",title:"Aerospace fields and ground infrastructure",html:"<p><a href='canaveral-aerospace-field.html'>Canaveral</a> is the principal operational field. Edwards anchors flight test and western recovery; Vandenberg supports high-inclination and polar work. Only a few sites possess the long reinforced runway, cryogenic stores, thermal-protection hangars, approach corridors, and rescue forces needed for normal orbital operations.</p>"},
      {id:"traffic",title:"From demonstration to fleet operations",html:"<p>Pathfinder proves the concept but does not make it useful. Columbia demonstrates lunar reach. Liberty establishes ordinary work, Hercules builds the station system, Ranger makes satellites serviceable, and Enterprise raises cadence.</p><p>By 1985 roughly forty orbital aircraft are operational, with another ten to fifteen in overhaul, trial, training, or reserve. About forty-five to sixty annual sorties constitute a real aerospace fleet far short of effortless airline service.</p>"},
      {id:"culture",title:"The astronaut as pilot-captain",html:"<p>American crews describe insertion, rendezvous, reentry, and landing through aviation language. Calculation remains indispensable—spaceplanes demand complex guidance, simulation, energy management, and abort planning—but the pilot is trusted inside a calculated envelope.</p><p>The vehicle name is therefore politically important. Pathfinder, Columbia, Liberty, Hercules, Ranger, and Enterprise present orbit as an extension of the national frontier rather than a domain of artillery.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Advantages and structural limits",html:"<p>Reusable vehicles support retrieval, repair, flexible payloads, crew rotation, and frequent access to lower orbit. They are less efficient for very heavy cargo and high-energy departure than large rockets assembled around the German Raumhafen.</p><p>American strategy compensates by specializing vehicles, building <a href='orbital-port-columbia.html'>Columbia</a> in accessible low orbit, and later moving Mars assembly to <a href='liberty-yard.html'>Liberty Yard</a>.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...vehicleRelated,{href:"space-programs.html",kicker:"Comparative chronology",label:"The Space Race"},{href:"project-pilgrim.html",kicker:"Mars successor",label:"Project Pilgrim"}],
    facts:[["First operational flight","OV-1A Pathfinder · 1969"],["Architecture","Self-powered horizontal takeoff and landing"],["Operating service","United States Aerospace Force"],["General transport","Liberty and Enterprise"],["Heavy transport","Hercules"],["Satellite service","Ranger"],["Operational fleet, 1985","Roughly 40 aircraft"],["Annual sorties, 1985","Approximately 45–60"]]
  }),

  "ov-1a-pathfinder": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"OV-1A Pathfinder",category:"Space vehicles",eyebrow:"First American runway-to-orbit aircraft · 1969",infoboxKicker:"Experimental orbital vehicle",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/american-spaceplane-lineage.svg",alt:"American orbital aircraft lineage with Pathfinder at its beginning",caption:"Pathfinder proves the architecture from which later operational families diverge"},
    lead:"The OV-1A Pathfinder is the first American aircraft to take off from a runway under its own power, enter orbit as one airframe, return through the atmosphere, and land horizontally. Its 1969 flight proves the national aerospace theory but remains an experimental achievement rather than routine transportation.",
    canon:"The designation, 1969 milestone, runway-to-orbit architecture, experimental role, limited payload, and influence on later vehicles are established. Crew complement, dimensions, mass, engine arrangement, individual airframe names, and flight count remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"development",title:"Development",html:"<p>Pathfinder grows from the post-1957 National Aero-Space Plane effort, joining high-speed aircraft laboratories, integrated-circuit guidance, cryogenic propulsion, ramjet research, and lifting-body experience. It is designed to prove a complete operating cycle rather than to maximize payload.</p>"},
      {id:"configuration",title:"Configuration",html:"<p>The aircraft carries every penalty of the national choice: wings, landing gear, large inlets, thermal protection, internal oxidizer, and transition machinery. It must survive powered atmospheric acceleration, orbital vacuum, hypersonic reentry, and an unpowered or lightly powered runway approach in one structure.</p>"},
      {id:"flight",title:"The 1969 orbital flight",html:"<p>The first complete mission begins and ends at an American aerospace field. Public coverage emphasizes continuous identity: the aircraft shown leaving the runway is the aircraft shown returning. That image distinguishes the achievement from booster-launched gliders and capsules.</p>"},
      {id:"computing",title:"Guidance, simulation, and computing",html:"<p>Pathfinder relies upon an integrated-circuit flight computer, inertial navigation, fixed mission software, telemetry, ground simulation, and carefully bounded pilot authority. The program helps make real-time computing and fault-tolerant instrumentation national industrial priorities.</p>"},
      {id:"pilots",title:"Pilot qualification",html:"<p>Test pilots are trained as systems operators as well as aviators. They manage energy state, propulsion transitions, thermal limits, abort corridors, and reentry geometry while accepting that many apparent choices are constrained by precomputed envelopes.</p>"},
      {id:"utility",title:"Limited operational utility",html:"<p>The early aircraft carries light experiments and qualification payloads. Its maintenance burden, narrow margins, and modest orbital capacity prevent it from serving as the mature station transport imagined by public rhetoric.</p><p>Pathfinder proves that the American theory works; it does not yet make the theory economical.</p>"},
      {id:"successors",title:"Columbia and Liberty successors",html:"<p>The <a href='columbia-spaceplane-program.html'>Columbia</a> lunar pair inherits its integrated ascent and reentry method. <a href='liberty-spaceplane.html'>Liberty</a> simplifies the achievement into a repeatable crew-and-cargo system. Later vehicles retain Pathfinder's operational culture even when their structures and roles diverge.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Public and institutional legacy",html:"<p>Pathfinder turns the American astronaut into a pilot-captain and gives the Aerospace Force a founding myth independent of the German rocket tradition. Surviving airframes later serve in training, reserve, research, or museum roles rather than defining normal orbital traffic.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...vehicleRelated,{href:"columbia-spaceplane-program.html",kicker:"Lunar successor",label:"Columbia Spaceplane Program"},{href:"rocket-awakening.html",kicker:"Political culture",label:"American Rocket Awakening"}],
    facts:[["Designation","OV-1A"],["Name","Pathfinder"],["First orbital flight","1969"],["Type","Experimental runway-to-orbit aerospace plane"],["Takeoff","Horizontal and self-powered"],["Landing","Horizontal runway recovery"],["Principal role","Proof and pilot qualification"],["Detailed specifications","Open"]]
  }),

  "columbia-spaceplane-program": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"Columbia Spaceplane Program",category:"Space vehicles",eyebrow:"OV-2A Columbia and OV-2B Columbia Tanker · 1971",infoboxKicker:"American lunar-flight system",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/columbia-lunar-mission.svg",alt:"Diagram of the 1971 Columbia lunar mission with two runway-launched vehicles and orbital refueling",caption:"OV-2B refuels OV-2A in low Earth orbit; Columbia then lands on the Moon and returns directly to Canaveral"},
    lead:"The Columbia Spaceplane Program is the two-aircraft system that lands Americans on the Moon on 20 July 1971. Both OV-2A Columbia and OV-2B Columbia Tanker take off independently from Canaveral; the tanker refuels Columbia in low Earth orbit, after which the lunar aircraft lands vertically on the Moon and returns directly to a runway on Earth.",
    canon:"The two-vehicle architecture, designations, mission date, orbital refueling, two-person lunar crew, vertical lunar landing, direct Earth return, and runway recovery are authorially fixed. Exact dimensions, masses, payloads, propellant quantities, flight profile, and individual tanker-crew names remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"deadline",title:"Kennedy's lunar deadline",html:"<p>President Kennedy's 1961 commitment gives the reusable program a destination and a date. Germany wins the first landing in December 1970 with an austere direct-ascent mission, leaving America to demonstrate a technically and politically distinct claim rather than merely repeat the feat.</p>"},
      {id:"pair",title:"A two-spaceplane mission",html:"<p>OV-2A Columbia is the lunar aircraft. OV-2B Columbia Tanker is a separately launched orbital propellant carrier. Both depart from Canaveral during the same mission window and rendezvous in low Earth orbit.</p><p>The mission has no separate first-flight lunar lander and no orbital tug. Later tugs and freight landers do not belong to the 1971 architecture.</p>"},
      {id:"refueling",title:"Orbital refueling",html:"<p>The tanker transfers the propellant needed for translunar injection, lunar descent and ascent, Earth return, and reserves. Docking and cryogenic transfer are therefore as central to the achievement as the landing itself.</p><p>The tanker crew becomes part of the national story despite never stepping onto the Moon. Its work makes the landing possible.</p>"},
      {id:"landing",title:"Lunar landing",html:"<p>Columbia carries two Americans to the lunar surface and lands vertically on its closed-cycle rocket system. Wings and runway landing gear are useless mass on the Moon, but carrying them is the point of the demonstration: one American aircraft travels from Florida to the lunar surface and back.</p>"},
      {id:"return",title:"Direct return to Canaveral",html:"<p>After lunar ascent Columbia leaves the Moon directly, reenters Earth's atmosphere, manages its hypersonic energy, and lands at <a href='canaveral-aerospace-field.html'>Canaveral Aerospace Field</a>. There is no transfer to a capsule or separate Earth-return craft.</p>"},
      {id:"date",title:"20 July 1971",html:"<p>The landing occurs seven months after Germany's first lunar arrival. Germany retains chronological priority; America claims reusable continuity, pilot command, and the return of the same aircraft.</p><p>Public language presents the mission as proof that Germany reached the Moon first but America has opened a road.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Inefficiency and risk",html:"<p>The mission pays heavily for wings, inlets, landing gear, and Earth thermal protection carried to lunar distance. Its architecture is not automatically the cheapest way to move later bulk freight or build a base.</p><p>Operational success does not erase limited fleet size, demanding inspection, or dependence upon perfect rendezvous and transfer.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>Columbia establishes orbital refueling and direct-return prestige, informs the general <a href='liberty-spaceplane.html'>Liberty</a> family, and gives its name to <a href='orbital-port-columbia.html'>Orbital Port Columbia</a>. Later American lunar logistics combine spaceplanes with heavy cargo, stations, and tugs rather than repeating the first mission unchanged.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...vehicleRelated,{href:"ov-1a-pathfinder.html",kicker:"Predecessor",label:"OV-1A Pathfinder"},{href:"kennedy-lunar-station.html",kicker:"Permanent successor",label:"Kennedy Lunar Station"}],
    facts:[["Lunar aircraft","OV-2A Columbia"],["Refueling aircraft","OV-2B Columbia Tanker"],["Mission date","20 July 1971"],["Launch method","Two independent runway takeoffs"],["Lunar crew","Two"],["Moon landing","Vertical"],["Earth return","Direct reentry"],["Final recovery","Canaveral runway"]]
  }),

  "liberty-spaceplane": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"Liberty Spaceplane",category:"Space vehicles",eyebrow:"OV-3A and OV-3B · routine orbital transport · 1972 onward",infoboxKicker:"General-purpose orbital aircraft family",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/american-spaceplane-lineage.svg",alt:"American orbital aircraft lineage highlighting the Liberty family",caption:"Liberty converts the Pathfinder and Columbia achievements into general crew, cargo, laboratory, and automatic service"},
    lead:"The Liberty is the first general-purpose American orbital aircraft family. OV-3A begins practical crew, cargo, satellite, and laboratory service in 1972; OV-3B Liberty Automatic adds fully automated cargo deployment and runway recovery in 1974.",
    canon:"The family name, OV-3A and OV-3B designations, 1972 and 1974 milestones, principal roles, use in Columbia construction, and later commercial transfer are established. Exact capacities, fleet totals, production blocks, range, and crew complement remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"purpose",title:"From prestige flight to routine work",html:"<p>Pathfinder proves runway-to-orbit flight and Columbia reaches the Moon. Liberty is designed around repeatable lower-orbit work: moving people, experiments, satellites, repair equipment, and useful cargo rather than winning one headline.</p>"},
      {id:"ov3a",title:"OV-3A Liberty",html:"<p>The crewed model supports transport, laboratory flights, satellite deployment, short servicing missions, and station assembly. Its mission bay and cabin are intended to accept changing payloads rather than one lunar profile.</p>"},
      {id:"ov3b",title:"OV-3B Liberty Automatic",html:"<p>The automatic model demonstrates fully uncrewed ascent, orbital cargo work, reentry, and runway recovery in 1974. It can place satellites, return experiments, and carry freight without consuming crew volume or accepting crew risk.</p>"},
      {id:"operations",title:"Operating cycle",html:"<p>Vehicles enter hangars for inspection of thermal protection, engines, landing gear, cryogenic systems, avionics, and structure. Reuse shortens the cycle relative to building a new spacecraft but never makes turnaround comparable to an ordinary airliner.</p>"},
      {id:"columbia",title:"Building Orbital Port Columbia",html:"<p>Liberty carries assembly crews, tools, life-support equipment, laboratories, and smaller modules during the 1978–79 construction campaign. Heavy elements travel aboard <a href='ov-4a-hercules.html'>Hercules</a>; Liberty makes those elements habitable and operational.</p>"},
      {id:"science",title:"Scientific and civil use",html:"<p>Universities, federal laboratories, firms, and foreign specialists fly through Space Science and Development Command. The aircraft is military-owned infrastructure used for civil work, and its manifests remain subject to service safety and security review.</p>"},
      {id:"commercial",title:"Commercial transfer",html:"<p>The <a href='commercial-orbital-transportation-act.html'>Commercial Orbital Transportation Act</a> permits licensed acquisition of complete older aircraft. Frontier Orbital Lines begins with two OV-3A vehicles, one OV-3B, parts, simulators, tooling, and a secondary Canaveral apron.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy and replacement",html:"<p>Enterprise becomes the preferred high-frequency transport after 1983, but Liberty remains useful in government, reserve, automatic freight, training, and private service. Its adaptable operating standard makes American commercial orbital transport possible.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...vehicleRelated,{href:"ov-6a-enterprise.html",kicker:"High-frequency successor",label:"OV-6A Enterprise"},{href:"frontier-orbital-lines.html",kicker:"Commercial operator",label:"Frontier Orbital Lines"}],
    facts:[["Crewed designation","OV-3A"],["Automatic designation","OV-3B"],["Crewed service","1972"],["Automatic milestone","1974"],["Primary roles","Crew · cargo · satellites · laboratories"],["Station role","Columbia assembly and support"],["Commercial user","Frontier Orbital Lines"],["Detailed capacity","Open"]]
  }),

  "ov-4a-hercules": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"OV-4A Hercules",category:"Space vehicles",eyebrow:"Heavy runway-to-orbit transport · 1976 onward",infoboxKicker:"Heavy orbital cargo aircraft",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/orbital-port-columbia-logistics.svg",alt:"Diagram showing Hercules lifting heavy station elements to Orbital Port Columbia",caption:"Hercules carries the structural mass; Liberty and Enterprise supply crews, equipment, and repeated service"},
    lead:"The OV-4A Hercules is the heavy-lift member of the American orbital aircraft fleet. Entering service in 1976, it carries station modules, trusses, propellant, bulk cargo, and other loads too large for the general Liberty family.",
    canon:"The designation, name, 1976 entry, heavy-transport role, Columbia construction work, and continued role in lunar and Mars logistics are established. Exact payload, dimensions, engine count, fleet strength, and production history remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"requirement",title:"The heavy-lift requirement",html:"<p>Routine reusable transport cannot by itself place large pressure vessels, trusses, tanks, radiators, reactors, shielding, and bulk stores into orbit. The Columbia decision turns heavy payload from an experimental concern into a scheduled logistics requirement.</p>"},
      {id:"design",title:"Specialized heavy airframe",html:"<p>Hercules trades the flexibility and cadence of smaller vehicles for greater internal volume and orbital delivery. It remains a runway-to-orbit aircraft rather than a vertical booster, preserving American operating identity at considerable structural and propulsion cost.</p>"},
      {id:"service",title:"Entry into service",html:"<p>The type enters service in 1976, one year before Congress authorizes Orbital Port Columbia. Its availability makes the rapid 1978–79 assembly campaign politically plausible.</p>"},
      {id:"construction",title:"Construction of Columbia",html:"<p>Four to six Hercules flights and several Liberty assembly flights establish the 1978 core working configuration. Hercules delivers the large elements; smaller vehicles deliver crews, connections, experiments, and repair material.</p>"},
      {id:"propellant",title:"Propellant and tanker work",html:"<p>Bulk propellant delivery supports station keeping, transfer stages, lunar logistics, and high-energy mission preparation. The vehicle serves as a carrier for tanks and depot equipment even where a dedicated tanker configuration is not fixed.</p>"},
      {id:"lunar",title:"Lunar logistics",html:"<p>Kennedy Station's continued occupation depends upon more than passenger flights. Hercules supports the equipment, shielding, power, rovers, drilling systems, and stores that turn an improvised outpost into a durable base.</p>"},
      {id:"pilgrim",title:"Project Pilgrim role",html:"<p>For Mars preparation Hercules places large components in lower orbit, after which tugs move selected assemblies toward <a href='liberty-yard.html'>Liberty Yard</a>. America thereby combines runway access with a separate high-orbit construction node.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Maintenance and limits",html:"<p>The heavy airframe is expensive to inspect and demanding upon runways, cryogenic handling, and approach corridors. It improves American mass flow without matching the largest German expendable boosters in single-launch scale.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...vehicleRelated,{href:"liberty-spaceplane.html",kicker:"Assembly partner",label:"Liberty Spaceplane"},{href:"liberty-yard.html",kicker:"High-orbit destination",label:"Liberty Yard"}],
    facts:[["Designation","OV-4A"],["Name","Hercules"],["Entered service","1976"],["Type","Heavy runway-to-orbit transport"],["Principal loads","Modules · trusses · propellant · bulk cargo"],["Major project","Orbital Port Columbia"],["Later program","Project Pilgrim"],["Detailed payload","Open"]]
  }),

  "ov-5a-ranger": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"OV-5A Ranger",category:"Space vehicles",eyebrow:"Satellite servicing and orbital rescue · 1979 onward",infoboxKicker:"Orbital service aircraft",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/american-spaceplane-lineage.svg",alt:"American orbital aircraft lineage highlighting Ranger",caption:"Ranger specializes the reusable fleet around objects and crews already in orbit"},
    lead:"The OV-5A Ranger is the American satellite-inspection, retrieval, repair, rescue, and high-cross-range orbital aircraft introduced in 1979. It turns reusability from a transportation advantage into a capacity to approach, manipulate, recover, and return other spacecraft.",
    canon:"The designation, 1979 entry, inspection, retrieval, repair, rescue, and high-cross-range missions are established. Exact sensors, manipulators, crew, payload, cross-range, and classified missions remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"requirement",title:"A service vehicle for a crowded orbit",html:"<p>By the late 1970s satellites, stations, debris, spent stages, and military inspectors make close approach an ordinary operational problem. General cargo aircraft can rendezvous, but a dedicated vehicle is needed for controlled inspection and work.</p>"},
      {id:"mobility",title:"Cross-range and orbital access",html:"<p>Ranger emphasizes maneuver, return flexibility, and the ability to reach objects not placed conveniently along ordinary transport routes. High cross-range also widens emergency landing and mission-abort options.</p>"},
      {id:"inspection",title:"Inspection",html:"<p>Declared work includes visual and instrument inspection of damaged, failed, or unidentified spacecraft. The same capability is strategically ambiguous because examination of a foreign military satellite can resemble preparation for interference.</p>"},
      {id:"repair",title:"Repair and retrieval",html:"<p>Crews replace accessible components, attach control packages, stabilize tumbling objects, retrieve experiments, or bring selected satellites back to Earth. Repair economics vary, but the political value of recovery is high.</p>"},
      {id:"rescue",title:"Rescue",html:"<p>Ranger can reach stranded crews, support a disabled vehicle, carry emergency life support, or return people from an unsafe orbit. Rescue duties later become part of the shared legal expectation created by the orbital conventions.</p>"},
      {id:"security",title:"Security implications",html:"<p>Orbital Security Command studies the type's approach and inspection experience while Project Zenith develops protected military derivatives. Ranger itself belongs to the general operational fleet, not automatically to the armed program.</p>"},
      {id:"law",title:"Incidents and close-approach rules",html:"<p>A dangerous 1981 inspection encounter demonstrates that technically controlled proximity can still be politically explosive. The <a href='geneva-orbital-incidents-agreement.html'>1982 Geneva agreement</a> requires notice of close approaches, transponders, safety zones, and direct communications.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<p>Ranger makes satellites repairable assets and orbital rescue a credible service rather than an improvised promise. Its dual-use mobility also helps make rules of encounter unavoidable.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...vehicleRelated,{href:"geneva-orbital-incidents-agreement.html",kicker:"Close-approach rules",label:"Geneva Orbital Incidents Agreement"},{href:"project-zenith.html",kicker:"Military derivative",label:"Project Zenith"}],
    facts:[["Designation","OV-5A"],["Name","Ranger"],["Entered service","1979"],["Primary role","Satellite servicing"],["Additional roles","Inspection · retrieval · repair · rescue"],["Flight characteristic","High cross-range"],["Legal consequence","1982 close-approach rules"],["Detailed specifications","Open"]]
  }),

  "ov-6a-enterprise": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"OV-6A Enterprise",category:"Space vehicles",eyebrow:"Second-generation orbital transport · 1983 onward",infoboxKicker:"High-frequency crew and cargo aircraft",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/american-spaceplane-lineage.svg",alt:"American orbital aircraft lineage culminating in Enterprise transport",caption:"Enterprise raises the cadence of a mature system rather than inaugurating it"},
    lead:"The OV-6A Enterprise is the second-generation American high-frequency crew-and-cargo orbital aircraft introduced in 1983. It consolidates the lessons of Pathfinder, Columbia, Liberty, Hercules, Ranger, and a decade of station operations into the principal routine transport of the mature fleet.",
    canon:"The designation, 1983 entry, high-frequency transport role, relationship to the older fleet, and contribution to 1985 sortie rates are established. Exact capacity, crew, production count, propulsion improvements, and retirement sequence remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"need",title:"Need for a second generation",html:"<p>Columbia's continuous occupation, Kennedy Station, growing satellite work, and planned Mars preparation demand a vehicle designed around repeated schedules rather than experimental flexibility. Liberty remains useful but carries the compromises of an earlier operating standard.</p>"},
      {id:"lessons",title:"Inherited lessons",html:"<p>Enterprise benefits from accumulated data on inlet transition, thermal cycles, structure, cryogenic handling, avionics, fault isolation, automated approach, and maintenance. Its novelty lies as much in reliability and operating organization as in raw performance.</p>"},
      {id:"mission",title:"Crew and cargo mission",html:"<p>The aircraft carries rotating crews, station stores, experiments, replacement equipment, and time-sensitive cargo. It does not replace Hercules for the largest modules or Ranger for specialized servicing.</p>"},
      {id:"cadence",title:"High-frequency service",html:"<p>Enterprise helps sustain roughly forty-five to sixty orbital sorties annually by 1985 across the total American fleet. “High frequency” remains relative to spacecraft: every mission still requires specialized fields, certified crews, inspection, and range control.</p>"},
      {id:"columbia",title:"Columbia traffic",html:"<p>Lower-orbit <a href='orbital-port-columbia.html'>Columbia</a> is optimized for precisely this service pattern. Enterprise unloads people and cargo into a port that can redistribute work to satellites, lunar flights, or high-orbit tugs.</p>"},
      {id:"kennedy",title:"Lunar rotation",html:"<p>Enterprise supports the increasingly regular Earth-orbit side of Kennedy Station logistics. Lunar transfer architecture may change by mission, but frequent access to Columbia makes the chain more resilient.</p>"},
      {id:"commercial",title:"Government and commercial era",html:"<p>The type remains government-operated in 1985 while older Liberty vehicles enter licensed private service. Its existence lets the Aerospace Force transfer less capable equipment without surrendering its newest national transport capacity.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Position in the 1985 fleet",html:"<p>Enterprise symbolizes the change from a sequence of heroic aircraft to a managed fleet. The American advantage is no longer a single landing; it is repeated movement, recovery, and the ability to assign different vehicles to different orbital tasks.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...vehicleRelated,{href:"liberty-spaceplane.html",kicker:"Predecessor",label:"Liberty Spaceplane"},{href:"project-pilgrim.html",kicker:"Strategic program",label:"Project Pilgrim"}],
    facts:[["Designation","OV-6A"],["Name","Enterprise"],["Entered service","1983"],["Generation","Second operational transport generation"],["Primary role","High-frequency crew and cargo"],["Principal destination","Orbital Port Columbia"],["Fleet context","About 40 operational orbital aircraft by 1985"],["Detailed specifications","Open"]]
  }),

  "canaveral-aerospace-field": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"Canaveral Aerospace Field",category:"Space infrastructure",eyebrow:"Principal American runway-to-orbit base · Florida",infoboxKicker:"United States Aerospace Force installation",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/columbia-lunar-mission.svg",alt:"Columbia lunar mission beginning and ending at Canaveral Aerospace Field",caption:"Canaveral supports two runway launches, orbital refueling, and the direct-return landing of the 1971 mission"},
    lead:"Canaveral Aerospace Field is the principal operating base of the American runway-to-orbit fleet. Its reinforced long runway, Atlantic corridors, cryogenic stores, thermal-protection hangars, mission control, and vehicle-sized aprons make it the ground end of the national orbital transportation system.",
    canon:"Canaveral's principal status, Florida location, long-runway architecture, cryogenic and maintenance functions, 1971 Columbia role, and later commercial apron are established. Exact runway dimensions, formal command name, field boundaries, and secondary facilities remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"selection",title:"Selection and expansion",html:"<p>Florida's Atlantic approaches, existing missile and tracking infrastructure, weather experience, and access to lower-inclination trajectories make Canaveral the natural operational center. The field expands from a launch range into an installation built for giant aircraft.</p>"},
      {id:"runway",title:"The long runway",html:"<p>A reinforced runway on the order of ten to twelve kilometers supports heavy takeoff, emergency braking, and demanding return margins. The exact completed length remains a specialist question, but the scale is a defining feature of the landscape.</p>"},
      {id:"propellant",title:"Cryogenic and propulsion complex",html:"<p>Buried or protected hydrogen and oxygen storage, transfer lines, fire separation, engine stands, and transition-system shops handle vehicles that combine atmospheric and rocket propulsion. Ground safety is part of every flight rather than a secondary airport function.</p>"},
      {id:"maintenance",title:"Thermal and structural maintenance",html:"<p>Hangars inspect heat protection, inlets, control surfaces, landing gear, pressure systems, avionics, and fatigue. Reuse concentrates labor in refurbishment and certification rather than eliminating the work of preparing a spacecraft.</p>"},
      {id:"control",title:"Mission and range control",html:"<p>Aerospace Force controllers coordinate airspace closure, ascent corridors, tracking, weather, orbital insertion, recovery, rescue, and alternate fields. Civil scientific missions remain inside this military traffic system.</p>"},
      {id:"columbia",title:"The 1971 Columbia mission",html:"<p>OV-2A and OV-2B depart Canaveral in the same mission window. After lunar flight, Columbia returns directly through Earth's atmosphere and lands on the field's runway, completing the central political claim of the program.</p>"},
      {id:"network",title:"Edwards, Vandenberg, and alternates",html:"<p>Edwards is the principal test and western recovery field; Vandenberg supports polar and high-inclination operations. Emergency runways widen abort options, but few can fully service an aircraft after landing.</p>"},
      {id:"commercial",title:"Commercial apron",html:"<p>The 1985 commercial settlement grants Frontier Orbital Lines a secondary apron, hangar access, tooling, and certified service arrangements. Private operation grows beside rather than outside the Aerospace Force installation.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...vehicleRelated,{href:"commercial-orbital-transportation-act.html",kicker:"Commercial law",label:"Commercial Orbital Transportation Act"},{href:"frontier-orbital-lines.html",kicker:"Private tenant",label:"Frontier Orbital Lines"}],
    facts:[["Location","Cape Canaveral, Florida"],["Operator","United States Aerospace Force"],["Primary function","Runway-to-orbit operations"],["Characteristic feature","Exceptionally long reinforced runway"],["Propellants","Cryogenic hydrogen and oxygen"],["Historic mission","Columbia lunar flight · 1971"],["Commercial tenant","Frontier Orbital Lines"],["Exact runway length","Open"]]
  }),

  "orbital-port-columbia": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"Orbital Port Columbia",category:"Space stations",eyebrow:"United States orbital port · authorized 1977 · occupied 1979",infoboxKicker:"American low-orbit station",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/orbital-port-columbia-logistics.svg",alt:"Logistics diagram for Orbital Port Columbia",caption:"Accessible low orbit, specialized orbital aircraft, and later tugs make Columbia a busy transfer and repair port"},
    lead:"Orbital Port Columbia is the principal American space station and orbital transport hub. Authorized in 1977 and continuously occupied from 1979, it is lower, busier, more modular, and easier to reach than Germany's Orbitaler Raumhafen.",
    canon:"The 1977 authorization, 1978 core assembly, 1979 continuous occupation, lower-orbit transport role, growth through 1985, Aerospace Force command, and later Frontier Annex are established. Exact orbit, dimensions, module names, and commander list remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"answer",title:"The American answer to the Raumhafen",html:"<p>Germany's 1975 harbor is a high-orbit shipyard supplied by enormous rockets. America declines to duplicate it. Columbia is placed in a more accessible orbit and designed around the national advantage in frequent reusable transport.</p>"},
      {id:"authorization",title:"Authorization in 1977",html:"<p>The National Mission and Productive Enterprise Act authorizes the port, lunar-base planning, fleet expansion, computing, and technical education. “Peace, production, and ascent” joins the station to Buchanan's wider mission state.</p>"},
      {id:"assembly",title:"Assembly, 1978–1979",html:"<p>Four to six <a href='ov-4a-hercules.html'>Hercules</a> flights and several <a href='liberty-spaceplane.html'>Liberty</a> assembly missions establish the 1978 core. Added habitation, laboratory, workshop, and life-support elements permit one crew to arrive before the previous crew leaves in 1979.</p>"},
      {id:"architecture",title:"Modular port architecture",html:"<p>Docking nodes, workshops, laboratories, surveillance systems, storage, construction areas, and experiment racks grow around an adaptable rather than monumental plan. A separated fission module later supplies dependable power without placing the reactor inside the primary inhabited cluster.</p>"},
      {id:"traffic",title:"Traffic and work",html:"<p>Columbia receives crews, cargo, satellites, damaged equipment, experiments, and mission specialists. It supports retrieval, repair, astronomy, Earth observation, experimental manufacturing, construction, and redistribution toward lunar or high-orbit missions.</p>"},
      {id:"command",title:"Military command and civil use",html:"<p>Orbital Transportation Command controls docking and traffic; Space Science and Development Command operates major research sections. Civilian occupants work on an Aerospace Force post whose commander controls life support, communications, rescue, and emergency action.</p>"},
      {id:"population",title:"Growth through 1985",html:"<p>From a normal crew of roughly eight around 1980, the station grows toward sixteen to twenty permanent occupants during 1981–84. Numbers vary with construction, visiting aircraft, and mission rotations.</p>"},
      {id:"later",title:"Liberty Yard and Frontier Annex",html:"<p>High-energy Mars assembly moves toward <a href='liberty-yard.html'>Liberty Yard</a>, reached by tug from Columbia. After 1993 Frontier Orbital becomes a National Orbital Carrier, and the private Frontier Annex joins the port during 1994–96.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...vehicleRelated,{href:"orbital-stations.html",kicker:"Comparative infrastructure",label:"Orbital Stations"},{href:"liberty-yard.html",kicker:"High-orbit annex",label:"Liberty Yard"},{href:"frontier-orbital-lines.html",kicker:"Later commercial partner",label:"Frontier Orbital Lines"}],
    facts:[["Authorized","1977"],["Core assembly","1978"],["Continuous occupation","1979"],["Operator","United States Aerospace Force"],["Primary role","Transport · repair · research · construction"],["Normal population, 1981–84","Approximately 16–20"],["Power addition","Separated fission module"],["Commercial annex","Frontier Annex · 1994–96"]]
  }),

  "kennedy-lunar-station": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"Kennedy Lunar Station",category:"Lunar bases",eyebrow:"First continuously occupied human outpost beyond Earth · 1979–1980",infoboxKicker:"American lunar station",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/orbital-port-columbia-logistics.svg",alt:"American logistics chain from Canaveral through Columbia to Kennedy Lunar Station",caption:"Frequent orbital traffic sustains a smaller and initially improvised lunar outpost"},
    lead:"Kennedy Lunar Station is the first continuously occupied human settlement beyond Earth. Established in late 1979 or 1980, it wins permanence for the United States with a smaller, improvised, frequently supplied base before Germany opens the larger standardized Mondhafen Süd.",
    canon:"The name, late-1979-or-1980 continuous-occupation milestone, political dedication to John F. Kennedy, improvised architecture, development through 1985, and Project Pilgrim laboratory role are established. Exact landing site, opening day, first crew, and module register remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"race",title:"The race for permanence",html:"<p>Germany lands first and builds the stronger heavy-transport architecture. America chooses a narrower milestone: one crew must arrive before the previous crew leaves. Frequent resupply and modular improvisation substitute for initial scale.</p>"},
      {id:"name",title:"Naming the station",html:"<p>Buchanan names the outpost for John F. Kennedy, whose 1961 commitment gave American spaceflight its coherent mission. The choice claims activist Democratic-Republican history as national inheritance and makes opposition appropriations politically difficult.</p>"},
      {id:"first",title:"First continuously occupied outpost",html:"<p>Continuous occupation begins in late 1979 or during 1980. The exact ceremonial opening remains unresolved, but the operational criterion is clear: no gap separates resident crews.</p>"},
      {id:"architecture",title:"Improvised frontier architecture",html:"<p>Early modules come from different programs and are connected under pressure. Quarters, laboratories, stores, power, communications, and emergency shelters look less standardized than the later German base.</p><p>The resulting visual culture is one of patched systems, checklists, dust, and visible adaptation.</p>"},
      {id:"growth",title:"Growth, 1981–1984",html:"<p>The station grows toward roughly ten to fourteen permanent personnel. Regolith-covered quarters, reactor power, laboratories, a machine shop, pressurized rovers, oxygen experiments, and a northern drilling camp make it more than a flag outpost.</p>"},
      {id:"work",title:"Scientific and industrial work",html:"<p>Crews study reduced gravity, geology, radiation, medicine, repair, excavation, landing pads, regolith shielding, drilling, and experimental oxygen or water recovery. Early industry reduces resupply and prepares later missions; it does not create self-sufficiency.</p>"},
      {id:"pilgrim",title:"Project Pilgrim laboratory",html:"<p>Kennedy becomes the human-systems laboratory for Mars. Long isolation, closed life support, nuclear power, maintenance, drilling, reduced-gravity medicine, rovers, and field repair can be tested with rescue still closer than Mars.</p>"},
      {id:"comparison",title:"Comparison with Mondhafen Süd",html:"<p>Germany's 1981 base is larger, standardized, and earlier in useful material processing. Kennedy remains smaller but benefits from higher American traffic. The two bases embody the wider contrast between German outward infrastructure and American repeated operations.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...vehicleRelated,{href:"orbital-stations.html",kicker:"German counterpart",label:"Orbitaler Raumhafen"},{href:"project-pilgrim.html",kicker:"Mars preparation",label:"Project Pilgrim"},{href:"catholic-pastoral-care-in-space.html",kicker:"Community life",label:"Catholic Pastoral Care in Space"}],
    facts:[["Named for","John F. Kennedy"],["Continuous occupation","Late 1979 or 1980"],["Historical distinction","First permanent human outpost beyond Earth"],["Operator","Space Science and Development Command"],["Character","Modular and improvised"],["Population, 1981–84","Approximately 10–14"],["Power","Compact fission reactor"],["Mars role","Project Pilgrim human-systems laboratory"]]
  }),

  "project-pilgrim": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"Project Pilgrim",category:"Space programs",eyebrow:"American Mars program · announced early 1980s",infoboxKicker:"Repeatable Mars transportation program",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/orbital-port-columbia-logistics.svg",alt:"Project Pilgrim logistics from Earth through Columbia and Liberty Yard toward Mars",caption:"Pilgrim separates frequent lower-orbit transport from high-orbit assembly and interplanetary departure"},
    lead:"Project Pilgrim was the federal American program that developed the people, surveys, life-support work, lifting-body research, and orbital infrastructure later used by the corporate Mars program. The Open Door Depression ended federal ownership as its organizing principle in 1990, but Frontier and allied firms carried the program into the first American Mars landing in 1999.",
    canon:"The program name, early-1980s adoption, original before-end-of-1996 objective, repeatable-system doctrine, Columbia and Liberty Yard architecture, Kennedy tests, 1990 commercial transfer, 1994 reconnaissance, 1996 cargo landing, 1999 first crew, and continuing occupation around 2001 are established. Vehicle specifications, crew, consortium, financing, and exact mission dates remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"challenge",title:"Mars as the next systems test",html:"<p>Two-to-three-year missions, radiation, life support, confinement, heavy atmospheric entry, ascent, surface power, repair, and twenty-six-month launch windows make Mars categorically harder than the lunar race. No human landing is plausible by 1985.</p>"},
      {id:"answer",title:"The American answer to Marsplan 92",html:"<p>Germany targeted the first arrival with one integrated expedition ship assembled at the Raumhafen. America promised a landing and safe return before the end of 1996, but defined success as a transportation and settlement system that could fly again.</p>"},
      {id:"network",title:"A distributed precursor network",html:"<p>American orbiters, relays, weather stations, rovers, sample caches, and ascent demonstrations reduce uncertainty before a crew departs. Redundancy is treated as an operating doctrine rather than a collection of unrelated missions.</p>"},
      {id:"columbia",title:"Columbia as the traffic base",html:"<p>Spaceplanes move people, instruments, and components into <a href='orbital-port-columbia.html'>Orbital Port Columbia</a>. The station provides repair, storage, training, control, and redistribution but sits too low for efficient final assembly of a large Mars vehicle.</p>"},
      {id:"yard",title:"Liberty Yard",html:"<p>Tugs move selected heavy components and propellant from Columbia to <a href='liberty-yard.html'>Liberty Yard</a> in higher orbit. The yard serves as depot and assembly site away from the busiest station traffic.</p>"},
      {id:"moon",title:"Kennedy Station as proving ground",html:"<p>Lunar crews test closed systems, compact reactors, isolation, drilling, repair, medicine, rovers, and reduced-gravity work. The Moon becomes a laboratory, quarry prospect, and training ground rather than a completed objective.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Buchanan and Reagan",html:"<p>Buchanan frames Pilgrim as the next national mission. Reagan preserves it while arguing that constitutional institutions and competitive contractors, rather than permanent party direction, are the mechanism through which free people sustain great projects.</p>"},
      {id:"status",title:"From federal program to corporate settlement",html:"<p>By 1985 Pilgrim remained a preparation system. The 1990 crash transferred its usable inheritance into the commercial system. Corporate orbiters reached Mars in 1994, cargo landed in 1996, and the first crew established the American outpost west of Olympus in 1999.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...vehicleRelated,{href:"american-corporate-mars-program.html",kicker:"Post-1990 successor",label:"American Corporate Mars Program"},{href:"liberty-yard.html",kicker:"Assembly site",label:"Liberty Yard"},{href:"kennedy-lunar-station.html",kicker:"Human-systems laboratory",label:"Kennedy Lunar Station"},{href:"marsplan-92.html",kicker:"German rival",label:"Marsplan 92"}],
    facts:[["Adopted","Early 1980s"],["Original deadline","Before the end of 1996"],["Commercial reorganization","1990"],["Program doctrine","Repeatable transportation"],["Earth-orbit base","Orbital Port Columbia"],["Lunar test site","Kennedy Lunar Station"],["First American Mars crew","1999"],["Successor","Corporate settlement system"]]
  }),

  "liberty-yard": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"Liberty Yard",category:"Space stations",eyebrow:"High-orbit assembly and fuel depot · Project Pilgrim",infoboxKicker:"American interplanetary assembly site",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/orbital-port-columbia-logistics.svg",alt:"Logistics diagram connecting Orbital Port Columbia to Liberty Yard by tug",caption:"Liberty Yard removes large Mars assemblies from Columbia's busy low-orbit traffic"},
    lead:"Liberty Yard is the planned American high-orbit assembly and propellant depot for Project Pilgrim. It is not a second full station comparable to Columbia; it is a lightly inhabited or intermittently crewed construction node reached by tug from the lower orbital port.",
    canon:"The name, Project Pilgrim role, high-orbit location, assembly-and-fuel function, dependence on Columbia and tugs, and mid-1980s development are established. Exact orbit, completion date, permanent population, module design, and operating authority remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"need",title:"Why Columbia is insufficient",html:"<p>Columbia is optimized for frequent spaceplane access in lower orbit. That location is excellent for traffic and repair but inefficient as the final departure point for massive interplanetary vehicles.</p>"},
      {id:"concept",title:"A yard rather than a city",html:"<p>Liberty Yard consists of docking, truss, storage, propellant, power, control, and assembly functions. Habitation is limited to what construction and servicing require. Public comparisons to the Raumhafen exaggerate its independent capacity.</p>"},
      {id:"orbit",title:"High-orbit position",html:"<p>A higher orbit reduces some energy penalties for outward departure and separates dangerous assembly from Columbia's densest traffic. The exact altitude and inclination remain open pending final mission design.</p>"},
      {id:"tugs",title:"Tug connection",html:"<p>Spaceplanes deliver crews and components to Columbia. Orbital tugs then move selected heavy elements, tanks, and supplies to the yard. This later use of tugs must not be projected backward into the corrected 1971 Columbia lunar mission.</p>"},
      {id:"assembly",title:"Mars-vehicle assembly",html:"<p>Large structures, propulsion stages, radiation protection, habitats, return systems, and stores can be checked and joined away from the lower port. The yard supports a repeatable transportation system rather than one preassembled prestige craft.</p>"},
      {id:"fuel",title:"Propellant depot",html:"<p>Storage and transfer are as important as structural assembly. Hercules and other carriers place tanks or propellant into the lower system; tugs and depot equipment organize the higher-orbit inventory.</p>"},
      {id:"law",title:"Traffic, safety, and security",html:"<p>The yard remains subject to Aerospace Force traffic control, registration, passivation, rescue, and military-payload review. Its distance from Columbia reduces ordinary collision risk but complicates emergency response.</p>"},
      {id:"status",title:"Status by the mid-1980s",html:"<p>The yard belongs to the Pilgrim build-up and is not a mature independent settlement in 1985. Exact completion and first full interplanetary assembly remain future decisions.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...vehicleRelated,{href:"project-pilgrim.html",kicker:"Parent program",label:"Project Pilgrim"},{href:"orbital-port-columbia.html",kicker:"Lower-orbit hub",label:"Orbital Port Columbia"},{href:"ov-4a-hercules.html",kicker:"Heavy carrier",label:"OV-4A Hercules"}],
    facts:[["Parent program","Project Pilgrim"],["Type","High-orbit assembly and fuel depot"],["Relationship to Columbia","Reached by orbital tug"],["Primary work","Mars-vehicle assembly"],["Permanent population","Not established"],["Status in 1985","Developmental"],["Exact orbit","Open"],["First completed Mars vehicle","Open"]]
  }),

  "commercial-orbital-transportation-act": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"Commercial Orbital Transportation Act of 1985",category:"United States legislation",eyebrow:"Reagan administration · licensed private orbital transport",infoboxKicker:"United States federal statute",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/american-spaceplane-lineage.svg",alt:"American orbital aircraft lineage showing older Liberty vehicles entering commercial operation",caption:"The Act transfers selected complete aircraft while retaining public command of traffic, safety, rescue, and strategic review"},
    lead:"The Commercial Orbital Transportation Act of 1985 is the federal law permitting licensed private operators to acquire and operate complete Aerospace Force-derived runway-to-orbit aircraft. It creates a commercial carrier industry without privatizing orbital traffic control, rescue, range safety, or strategic-payload authority.",
    canon:"The year, complete-aircraft licensing, government reserved powers, Frontier Orbital foundation, and initial Liberty transfer are established. Exact statutory title form, vote, regulator, ownership caps, fees, insurance rules, and 1993 charter rights remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"background",title:"Background",html:"<p>By 1985 the Aerospace Force operates a newer generation while holding older but serviceable Liberty airframes, tooling, simulators, and an extensive contractor base. Reagan seeks private growth without dismantling the national program.</p>"},
      {id:"licenses",title:"Licensed complete aircraft",html:"<p>The statute allows approved companies to lease or purchase complete orbital aircraft and ground equipment. The operator must satisfy vehicle certification, crew qualification, maintenance, insurance, payload, range, and rescue requirements.</p>"},
      {id:"reserved",title:"Reserved government powers",html:"<p>The Aerospace Force retains airspace and range control, orbital traffic management, docking certification, rescue coordination, military-payload review, emergency requisition, and the power to refuse unsafe or strategic missions.</p>"},
      {id:"ownership",title:"Strategic ownership",html:"<p>Orbital launch remains protected infrastructure during Reagan's commercial opening. Foreign capital may obtain limited financial interests under rules not yet fully fixed, but operating control and strategic access remain American.</p>"},
      {id:"frontier",title:"Frontier Orbital Lines",html:"<p>Konrad Elias Voss forms <a href='frontier-orbital-lines.html'>Frontier Orbital Lines</a> under the Act. Its initial package contains two older OV-3A Liberty vehicles, one OV-3B automatic freighter, parts, simulators, tooling, and a secondary Canaveral apron.</p>"},
      {id:"market",title:"The first commercial market",html:"<p>Government anchor contracts, satellite deployment and retrieval, station freight, repair work, returned experiments, and corporate specialists provide demand before ordinary private passengers can support a carrier.</p>"},
      {id:"depression",title:"The Open Door Depression",html:"<p>Frontier survives the 1990 collapse because physical orbital transport remains necessary. A weakened government outsources more routine traffic, demonstrating that commercialization can deepen during fiscal crisis rather than disappear.</p>"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy and unresolved charter",html:"<p>Harwood designates Frontier a National Orbital Carrier in 1993 and the Frontier Annex later joins Columbia. The precise rights, obligations, and ownership treatment of that charter remain open and should not be inferred from the 1985 statute.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"frontier-orbital-lines.html",kicker:"Principal licensee",label:"Frontier Orbital Lines"},{href:"canaveral-aerospace-field.html",kicker:"Operating base",label:"Canaveral Aerospace Field"},{href:"liberty-spaceplane.html",kicker:"Transferred aircraft",label:"Liberty Spaceplane"},{href:"reagan-era.html",kicker:"Political setting",label:"Reagan Era"},{href:"orbital-law.html",kicker:"International framework",label:"Orbital Law"}],
    facts:[["Enacted","1985"],["President","Ronald Reagan"],["Permitted asset","Complete licensed orbital aircraft"],["Primary licensee","Frontier Orbital Lines"],["Initial transferred family","OV-3 Liberty"],["Traffic authority","Retained by Aerospace Force"],["Emergency power","Government requisition"],["Detailed ownership caps","Open"]]
  }),

  "frontier-orbital-lines": americanSpaceArticle({
    title:"Frontier Orbital Lines",category:"Commercial spaceflight",eyebrow:"American orbital carrier · founded 1985",infoboxKicker:"Licensed private orbital operator",
    flag:{src:"assets/diagrams/orbital-port-columbia-logistics.svg",alt:"Commercial orbital logistics connecting Frontier Orbital Lines with Columbia",caption:"Frontier begins on the government-built runway-to-orbit road and later adds specialized freight and crew vehicles"},
    lead:"Frontier Orbital Lines is the first durable private American orbital carrier. Founded by Konrad Elias Voss with older Liberty aircraft and government anchor contracts, it survives the Open Door Depression, develops specialized transports, and joins Orbital Port Columbia without escaping Aerospace Force traffic and security control.",
    canon:"The founder, 1985 Act and foundation, initial Liberty fleet, survival of the 1990 crash, Courier–Caravan–Wayfarer sequence, 1993 National Orbital Carrier charter, Frontier Annex, and Project Switchback are established. Detailed vehicle specifications, ownership, livery, and charter rights remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"founder",title:"Konrad Elias Voss",html:"<p>Voss is the American son of a neglected Sänger–Bredt engineer and understands both the spaceplane tradition and the political frustration of technical lines left outside the dominant state program. He presents Frontier as a carrier, not a rival national space agency.</p>"},
      {id:"act",title:"Foundation under the 1985 Act",html:"<p>The <a href='commercial-orbital-transportation-act.html'>Commercial Orbital Transportation Act</a> allows the company to acquire complete certified aircraft while leaving range, traffic, rescue, payload review, docking, and emergency requisition with the Aerospace Force.</p>"},
      {id:"fleet",title:"Initial Liberty fleet",html:"<p>Frontier begins with two older OV-3A Liberty vehicles, one OV-3B automatic freighter, spare parts, simulators, tooling, and a service lease at a secondary Canaveral apron. The transfer is substantial but does not include the newest Enterprise transport.</p>"},
      {id:"market",title:"Anchor contracts and early market",html:"<p>Satellite delivery and retrieval, Columbia freight, repairs, returned experiments, and corporate mission specialists provide dependable work. Government contracts stabilize the schedule while commercial customers learn to buy fixed-price payload space.</p>"},
      {id:"vehicles",title:"Specialized commercial vehicles",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Vehicle</th><th>Development</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Courier</td><td>1988–89</td><td>Urgent automatic satellite and return cargo</td></tr><tr><td>Caravan</td><td>1991–93</td><td>Bulk station freight</td></tr><tr><td>Wayfarer</td><td>1994–96</td><td>Crew and corporate specialists</td></tr><tr><td>Propellant tanker</td><td>Late 1990s</td><td>Routine orbital fuel logistics</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"depression",title:"Survival of the Open Door Depression",html:"<p>Financial collapse nearly destroys the company, but operating assets and necessary transport work retain value. Domestic capital replaces vulnerable holdings, and a fiscally weaker government outsources more routine Columbia traffic.</p>"},
      {id:"charter",title:"National carrier and Frontier Annex",html:"<p>President Harwood charters Frontier as a National Orbital Carrier in 1993. The Frontier Annex joins Columbia during 1994–96, embedding private freight, maintenance, and crew movement inside the national port.</p>"},
      {id:"switchback",title:"Project Switchback and Mars settlement",html:"<p>Switchback begins in 1993 as reusable vertical-rocket research and reaches intact orbital-class recovery around 2000–02. Limited scheduled bulk service follows while spaceplanes retain crew, servicing, delicate-payload, and return-cargo missions.</p><p>Frontier also organizes the commercial successor to Project Pilgrim, landing the first American Mars crew in 1999 and becoming the functional center of the national orbital transport system.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"konrad-voss.html",kicker:"Founder",label:"Konrad Elias Voss"},{href:"american-corporate-mars-program.html",kicker:"Planetary system",label:"American Corporate Mars Program"},{href:"commercial-orbital-transportation-act.html",kicker:"Enabling statute",label:"Commercial Orbital Transportation Act"},{href:"liberty-spaceplane.html",kicker:"Initial aircraft",label:"Liberty Spaceplane"},{href:"orbital-port-columbia.html",kicker:"Principal destination",label:"Orbital Port Columbia"},{href:"open-door-depression.html",kicker:"Survival crisis",label:"Open Door Depression"}],
    facts:[["Founded","1985"],["Founder","Konrad Elias Voss"],["Initial fleet","Two OV-3A; one OV-3B"],["Principal base","Canaveral secondary apron"],["National charter","1993"],["Columbia annex","1994–96"],["First American Mars crew","1999"],["Vertical bulk lift","Operational after 2000"]]
  })
});
